{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "meta": {
    "id": "art_modes-the-tritone",
    "slug": "modes-the-tritone",
    "title": "Where the Tritone Lands",
    "summary": "Every mode contains exactly one tritone — the tension inside the scale never changes, only which two degrees it sits on.",
    "tags": ["modes", "theory"],
    "readingTimeMin": 3,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-15"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The tritone is three whole tones, six frets, the widest and most unsettled interval a diatonic scale contains. You may already know it as the "
        },
        { "text": "b5", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": " of the blues scale — the same distance, just met in a different set of notes. Every one of the seven modes has exactly one, and where it lands is the last piece of structure this chapter has for you."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Not \"at least one\" — "
        },
        { "text": "exactly one", "marks": ["bold"] },
        {
          "text": " pair of degrees, six semitones apart, out of the 21 possible pairs in a seven-note scale. The seven modes are seven different note sets on "
        },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", so each one's tritone sits between a different pair of degrees." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "header": [[{ "text": "Mode" }], [{ "text": "Tritone degrees" }], [{ "text": "On A" }]],
      "rows": [
        [
          [{ "text": "Lydian" }],
          [
            { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and " },
            { "text": "#4", "marks": ["code", { "type": "color", "tone": "violet" }] }
          ],
          [{ "text": "A – D#", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "Ionian" }],
          [
            { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and " },
            { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] }
          ],
          [{ "text": "D – G#", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "Mixolydian" }],
          [
            { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and " },
            { "text": "b7", "marks": ["code", { "type": "color", "tone": "amber" }] }
          ],
          [{ "text": "C# – G", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "Dorian" }],
          [
            { "text": "b3", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and " },
            { "text": "6", "marks": ["code", { "type": "color", "tone": "amber" }] }
          ],
          [{ "text": "C – F#", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "Aeolian" }],
          [
            { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and " },
            { "text": "b6", "marks": ["code"] }
          ],
          [{ "text": "B – F", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "Phrygian" }],
          [
            { "text": "b2", "marks": ["code", { "type": "color", "tone": "rose" }] },
            { "text": " and " },
            { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] }
          ],
          [{ "text": "Bb – E", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ],
        [
          [{ "text": "Locrian" }],
          [
            { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
            { "text": " and " },
            { "text": "b5", "marks": ["code", { "type": "color", "tone": "rose" }] }
          ],
          [{ "text": "A – Eb", "marks": ["code"] }]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "It isn't the tritone's presence that varies from mode to mode — that's constant. It's its "
        },
        { "text": "address", "marks": ["bold"] },
        {
          "text": ": whether it touches the tonic, or the tonic triad, is what changes, and that's what to read off the table."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Ionian and Aeolian are the only two whose tritone misses the tonic triad completely — Ionian's triad is "
        },
        { "text": "1 3 5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and its tritone sits on " },
        { "text": "4", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "; Aeolian's triad is " },
        { "text": "1 b3 5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and its tritone sits on " },
        { "text": "2", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "b6", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ". That's the structural reason they're the two references everything else on the ladder is measured against: their home chord isn't touched by the least stable interval in the scale."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Every other mode's tritone reaches into its own tonic triad — Lydian's touches the "
        },
        { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", Mixolydian's the " },
        { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", Dorian's the " },
        { "text": "b3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", Phrygian's the " },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", and Locrian's both the " },
        { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and the " },
        { "text": "b5", "marks": ["code", { "type": "color", "tone": "rose" }] },
        { "text": " at once." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Mixolydian's row is the one worth stopping on. "
        },
        { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "b7", "marks": ["code", { "type": "color", "tone": "amber" }] },
        { "text": " are the two notes of a dominant seventh chord — " },
        { "text": "A7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is " },
        { "text": "A C# E G", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": ", and its " },
        { "text": "C#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code", { "type": "color", "tone": "amber" }] },
        {
          "text": " are exactly that tritone. That's why a dominant seventh chord and the Mixolydian mode are the same idea — "
        },
        { "text": "A7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " is the seventh chord this mode gives you." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "progression-player",
      "props": {
        "chords": ["A", "A7"],
        "bpm": 66,
        "caption": "The tonic triad, then the same chord with the b7 added — the C# and the G are the tritone"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Lydian's is measured off the root itself, "
        },
        { "text": "1", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " and " },
        { "text": "#4", "marks": ["code", { "type": "color", "tone": "violet" }] },
        {
          "text": " — the only one of the three major-home modes whose tritone touches the tonic at all, though it never reaches the "
        },
        { "text": "3", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " or the " },
        { "text": "5", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": "." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "And Locrian's is the striking one: home is itself a tritone. Its tonic triad, "
        },
        { "text": "A C Eb", "marks": ["code"] },
        {
          "text": ", contains both notes of its own unstable interval. What that does to Locrian's ability to function as a home is chapter 5's question; here it's just the address."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Try it against a held root: start an "
        },
        { "text": "A", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " on " },
        { "text": "Drone", "link": { "kind": "screen", "href": "/drone" } },
        { "text": ", play " },
        { "text": "C#", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " then " },
        { "text": "G", "marks": ["code", { "type": "color", "tone": "amber" }] },
        { "text": " against it, and hear the same pull an " },
        { "text": "A7", "marks": ["code"] },
        { "text": " chord has." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "That closes out the technical run of this chapter. You now have an exact ordering, an exact account of the one note that separates every neighbouring pair, two families with two dials each, and the two structural facts underneath the sound — where the half steps fall, and where the tritone lands. One question is left, and it's the honest one: does any of this actually predict what a mode "
        },
        { "text": "sounds", "marks": ["italic"] },
        { "text": " like?" }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
